<article class="env-lemma" id="08YD">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08YD">Lemma <span data-tag="08YD">5.17.7</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ be a continuous map of topological spaces. If $X$ is quasi-compact and $Y$ is Hausdorff, then $f$ is universally closed. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since every point of $Y$ is closed, we see from Lemma <a href="/tag/005C" data-tag="005C">5.12.3</a> that the closed subset $f^{-1}(y)$ of $X$ is quasi-compact for all $y \in Y$. Thus, by Theorem <a href="/tag/005R" data-tag="005R">5.17.5</a> it suffices to show that $f$ is closed. If $E \subset X$ is closed, then it is quasi-compact (Lemma <a href="/tag/005C" data-tag="005C">5.12.3</a>), hence $f(E) \subset Y$ is quasi-compact (Lemma <a href="/tag/04Z9" data-tag="04Z9">5.12.7</a>), hence $f(E)$ is closed in $Y$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/08YB" data-tag="08YB">5.12.4</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>